<ModellingLayout title="Context-map records: make the agreement between communities explicit" description="A context map explains relationships between models and their owners. It does not replace term mappings, describe network topology or make every participant use the same definition." chapter="Connect without collapsing" claim="method">
  <section aria-labelledby="two-layers">
    <h2 id="two-layers"><span id="two-scales" aria-hidden="true"></span><span id="worked-example" aria-hidden="true"></span>Two layers answer two different questions</h2>
    <p>A strategic context map asks how bounded contexts relate: where influence or dependency runs, how participants collaborate, and whether a downstream context translates an upstream language to protect its own model. A term mapping asks how two particular concepts correspond. Neither question can be answered by substituting the other’s diagram.</p>
    <p>For the Harbour Court illustration, imagine a surveying context supplying inspection descriptions to a transaction-assessment context. The strategic relationship might describe how they agree a published language and who owns changes to it. A particular mapping might then relate a surveying concept to an assessment concept. The existence of the collaboration does not prove that either concept is an exact match.</p>
    <p>The example is a teaching scenario, not an assertion that OPDA has adopted these context boundaries, collaboration patterns or mapping records. The real boundary must be established through practitioner language, identity criteria and accountable model ownership.</p>
  </section>

<figure class="modelling-figure" data-modelling-figure="context-mapping-bridge">
    <ModellingVisual kind="context-mapping-bridge" />
    <ol class="modelling-steps">
      <li><strong>Active map projection.</strong> The chosen current-state or target-state view scopes its relationships.</li>
      <li><strong>Qualified strategic relationship.</strong> Named participants, relationship kind and any side-specific patterns.</li>
      <li><strong>Exact applicability bridge.</strong> A link to an individual mapping record that applies at this seam.</li>
      <li><strong>Term correspondence.</strong> Two terms, a SKOS predicate and a qualified SSSOM record.</li>
    </ol>
    <figcaption>Mapping subject/object order need not match upstream/downstream direction. A strategic seam is not itself an SSSOM mapping.</figcaption>
  </figure>

  <section aria-labelledby="seam">
    <h2 id="seam"><span id="boundary" aria-hidden="true"></span><span id="patterns" aria-hidden="true"></span><span id="record" aria-hidden="true"></span>Qualify a relationship in the map where it holds</h2>
    <p>The selected context-map profile gives an active map projection an identity and gives each strategic relationship a named record. The record belongs to exactly one map. This prevents an assertion from a target-state proposal being silently combined with a current-state relationship as though both described one unqualified arrangement.</p>
    <p>Use a relationship kind that expresses the actual agreement. Partnership and Shared Kernel are symmetric forms; Customer/Supplier and generic Upstream/Downstream are directed forms. Separate Ways records an explicit decision not to integrate. The record’s participant structure must match the kind: a symmetric relationship must not acquire an invented upstream side merely to fit an arrow template.</p>
    <p>For directed relationships, the source profile allows upstream patterns such as Published Language and Open Host Service, and downstream responses such as Anti-Corruption Layer or Conformist. An anti-corruption layer here means preserving a downstream model through a deliberate translation boundary. It is not a cybersecurity assertion and does not specify an API gateway, message broker or deployment pattern.</p>
    <p>Internal and external participation are explicit relative to one map. An organisation may appear external to one projection and internal to another. Do not infer externality from a different hostname or from a missing “internal” flag.</p>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="mapping-join">
    <h2 id="mapping-join"><span id="term-mappings" aria-hidden="true"></span>Connect a seam to the mapping that actually applies</h2>
    <p>A mapping set may contain correspondences for several context pairs. Linking a seam only to the whole set can return mappings unrelated to its participants. The required bridge therefore identifies individual SSSOM mapping records. The question becomes “which exact reviewed correspondences apply here?”, not “which file happens to contain something relevant?”.</p>
    <p>Each mapping’s subject and object source identify a vocabulary or identifier system. A context’s authority over those systems is explicit, governed information. The profile checks that the systems supplying the mapping endpoints are compatible with the seam’s endpoint contexts. String-prefix similarity is not evidence of that authority.</p>
    <p>A namespace-based identifier system records its namespace as structured metadata and is explicitly typed as that specialised kind of system. Other identifier systems need not be namespace-based. This distinction allows a source identifier to mean what SSSOM intends rather than overloading it as a context label, a convenient URL prefix or an application name.</p>
    <p>A valid seam can have no applicable mapping yet; a reviewed mapping can exist without a strategic seam. These are reviewable states, not automatic defects. What is prohibited is inventing correspondence or applicability from a map’s visual proximity.</p>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="negative">
    <h2 id="negative">Absence is not an explicit decision</h2>
    <p>A map with no Shared Kernel relationship does not establish that Shared Kernel was considered and rejected. The selected profile can record a source-backed, map-wide exclusion as a positive assertion. That exclusion is different from Separate Ways, which concerns a particular relationship between named participants.</p>
    <p>The exclusion identifies the exact map/kind statement and its evidence; generic evidence attached somewhere on a map is insufficient. Validation can then reject a member relationship that contradicts the explicit exclusion. It must not manufacture exclusions by counting zero rows.</p>
    <p>This is the practical importance of the open-world distinction: “not recorded here”, “explicitly excluded by this map” and “these two contexts chose Separate Ways” are three different statements with different evidence requirements.</p>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="history">
    <h2 id="history">Respect this profile’s deliberate history boundary</h2>
    <p>The 30 August source amendment makes these context maps active-projection data only. Current-state and target-state projections can coexist; historical assertions do not accumulate in the RDF model. Files and version control preserve changes. A ContextRelationship must not acquire direct <code>prov:wasDerivedFrom</code> or <code>prov:wasRevisionOf</code> assertions in this profile.</p>
    <p>There is no replacement relationship-lineage, predecessor, supersession or currentness model to add “for completeness”. This is a deliberately scoped architecture decision, not a denial that provenance and history matter elsewhere. The <a href="/semantic-modelling/method/evidence-and-time">inspection and report timeline</a> concerns different resources and must not be copied indiscriminately onto strategic relationships.</p>
    <p>Named graphs are likewise not substitutes for context identity or map semantics. Graph membership is a storage or provenance convention; model ownership comes from the authoritative context registry and namespace policy. A diagram and a JSON representation should be projections of the same asserted map, not independently edited versions of the agreement.</p>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="failure">
    <h2 id="failure">Failure case: a beautiful map with the wrong join</h2>
    <p>Suppose a surveying-to-assessment arrow points at a mapping set containing surveying, lending and energy-assessment mappings. A consumer joins every member of the set to the arrow and displays them all as applicable. Nothing about the diagram reveals the error; the join was too broad.</p>
    <p>The repair is assertion-level applicability and endpoint authority checking. Inspect the individual mapping record, the identifier systems of its endpoints and the contexts governing those systems. Preserve its mapping direction independently from the DDD relationship direction. Do not “repair” the result by renaming terms or changing their identifiers to resemble the arrow labels.</p>
    <p>Read <a href="/semantic-modelling/method/mapping-records">mapping records</a> for the correspondence contract, or the introductory <a href="/semantic-modelling/explore/contexts-and-connections">contexts and connections</a> explanation.</p>
  </section>

